course: dictionary
lesson: 8273
title: Rightly and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Rightly means correctly or truly, or fairly, for a good reason. «She was rightly proud of her science project» means she had a good reason to feel proud.
explain: Look at the words around rightly to find its meaning. In «rightly so», the speaker agrees that something is fair. «Quite rightly» works the same way.
explain: Rightly can also be used in «rightly or wrongly», which means whether something is right or wrong. «If I remember rightly» means if my memory is correct.
explain: In the book, «where heroes could fitly feast after victory» uses fitly, which means the same as rightly. Swap rightly into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Rightly means correctly or truly.
fact: Rightly also means fairly, for a good reason.
fact: «Rightly or wrongly» means whether something is right or wrong.
fact: «Rightly so» and «quite rightly» show that the speaker agrees something is fair.
fact: In the book, «fitly» means the same as rightly.
exercise: «She was rightly proud of her science project.» What does rightly mean here? | Quietly | Wrongly and unfairly | Very slowly | *Fairly, for a good reason
exercise: «If I remember rightly, the library opens at nine.» What does rightly mean here? | Quickly | Late | *Correctly | Loudly
exercise: Mum says «rightly so» after Ben says sorry. What does she mean? | Ben was late for school | Ben should not feel proud | *Ben's apology was fair and deserved | Ben was wrong to say sorry
test: In the book, «fitly» means the same as which word? | Lazily | Loudly | Quickly | *Rightly
test: «Rightly or wrongly, Sam decided to tell the truth.» What does this phrase mean? | *It means whether his choice was right or wrong | Sam was very loud | Sam was definitely right | Sam was sad about the truth
exam: Which sentence uses rightly to mean correctly or truly? | *Tom, if I remember rightly, you live on Maple Road | The rightly cat slept on the mat | Tom ran rightly down the hill | Tom was rightly hungry at lunch
